course: dictionary
lesson: 4911
title: Imitate: copy and pretend
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To imitate something is to copy it. You can imitate a voice, a movement or a sound. «A gull stamps its feet on grass to imitate rain», so the sound of rain makes worms come up where the gull can catch them.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. «The King decides that the Knave must have imitated someone's handwriting» tells you that imitating means copying another person's writing, and the King thinks the copy was made to fool people.
explain: Try your meaning in the sentence. «An app that imitates a famous person» is a bot copying a style, but the person is not there. Imitate means to copy so that something looks or sounds like the real thing, without being the real thing.
fact: Imitate means to copy the way someone speaks, moves or behaves.
fact: Imitate can also mean to make something look or sound like something else.
fact: To imitate someone as an example means to follow them, as in «imitate your heroes».
fact: «Today's chatbots only imitate feelings in words» means they copy the way feelings are written, but do not really feel them.
fact: Imitate, imitates, imitated and imitating are all forms of the same verb.
exercise: «A gull stamps its feet on grass to imitate rain.» What does imitate mean here? | Wash its feet in the grass | *Make something look or sound like rain | Dig a hole in the ground | Fly away from the rain
exercise: Mia ___ her big sister's funny walk to make everyone laugh. Which verb fits the gap? | hides | *imitates | paints | forgets
exercise: Why might some animals imitate other animals? | To get lost in the forest | To learn to sing opera | *To stay safe | To make the other animals cry
test: «The King decides that the Knave must have imitated someone's handwriting.» What does imitated mean? | Threw the paper away | Read aloud very slowly | *Copied so it looks like someone else's | Forgot how to write
test: Which phrase means follow someone as an example? | hide your heroes | forget your heroes | *imitate your heroes | lose your heroes
exam: «Today's chatbots only imitate feelings in words.» What does this tell you about chatbots? | They only write numbers and never words | They really feel sad and happy like people do | *They copy the way feelings are written, without really feeling them | They read feelings out of library books
